WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.075 --> 00:00:01.682
(upbeat music)
(audience cheering)

00:00:01.682 --> 00:00:02.987
- All right. Welcome back, everybody.

00:00:02.987 --> 00:00:06.480
We love shining a light on
people who use their imaginations

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:08.370
to open opportunities for others,

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:10.410
so do our friends at Pilot
Pen, makers of Frixion,

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:12.300
the go-to erasable pen of
"The Kelly Clarkson Show."

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:15.600
And that is why we want to
introduce you to our next guest.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:17.310
She has a screen printing company

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:19.140
that not only employs autistic people,

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:22.380
but gives them opportunities
to create fearlessly as well.

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:25.404
From The Awesome Company,
everybody, welcome Jacquie.

00:00:25.404 --> 00:00:28.438
(audience cheering)
(audience applauding)

00:00:28.438 --> 00:00:29.638
So-
- Oh my gosh.

00:00:29.638 --> 00:00:31.050
- Thank you for being here. We're so-

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:31.883
- What is happening?
- So excited.

00:00:31.883 --> 00:00:33.797
- I'm so happy to be here. You're so cool.

00:00:33.797 --> 00:00:35.430
- Ditto. I am not that cool.

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:36.510
You will find out.

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:39.180
But I hear that the inspiration
for this, everything,

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:40.050
is your son, right?

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:41.550
- Yes.
- Yeah, so what happened?

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:42.420
- He is older now,

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:45.368
and so I really think that
I am now my own inspiration.

00:00:45.368 --> 00:00:46.201
Seriously.
- I love this.

00:00:46.201 --> 00:00:47.580
Yeah.
- I'm an autism mom,

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:51.180
and I navigated that world
like a lot of other people.

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:55.230
And I noticed that the
autistic kids had a path.

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:57.870
It's not clear sometimes,
it's a little bumpy,

00:00:57.870 --> 00:00:59.460
but it definitely goes into a line.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:04.080
We know what resources they
need, support, friends.

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:05.550
But what I noticed is, you know,

00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:08.010
autistic kids become autistic adults,

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:12.840
and what happens, and what
I saw happen is nothing.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:17.190
And some of the individual's
lives became smaller

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:18.990
and opportunities were kind of bleak.

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:21.210
And I was like, seriously, just over it.

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:23.700
And so I was like, "Well,
I'm starting something.

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:26.430
I don't know what I'm
doing, but I don't care."

00:01:26.430 --> 00:01:29.707
So I bought a T-shirt
printing machine and I said,

00:01:29.707 --> 00:01:32.700
"I'm hiring people that have autism

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:34.050
and we're gonna print shirts for people,

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:34.980
and we're gonna sell 'em.

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:36.810
And the more T-shirts we sell,

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:38.340
the more autistic people I'm gonna hire.

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:40.040
And that's just what I'm gonna do."

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:41.436
So I just did it.

00:01:41.436 --> 00:01:43.019
- And it's awesome.
(audience cheering)

00:01:43.019 --> 00:01:44.100
It's incredible.

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:45.341
And that's a powerful statement,

00:01:45.341 --> 00:01:48.416
because not everybody
knows how to do that.

00:01:48.416 --> 00:01:49.890
I think you have the idea,
but you don't actually...

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:50.910
It doesn't come to fruition.

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:51.840
That's why I think it's incredible.

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:53.640
So let's talk about your employees though,

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:54.690
'cause I do wanna know,

00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:55.870
you're only able to hire so many, right?

00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:57.300
- Yeah.
- So that's kind of

00:01:57.300 --> 00:01:59.340
one of the issues is you're
wanting to hire more,

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:01.500
but you found a way to
navigate around that, right?

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:03.990
- Yes, well, I was kind of at a crossroads

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:07.140
where I have a great group
of people that work for me,

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:10.500
and we have about 12 full-time employees.

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:12.960
And do I invest in them

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:14.640
or do I just keep bringing more people in?

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:16.260
No, I'm gonna invest in my team

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:18.870
because I want them to
feel valued, respected,

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:20.670
safe, important.

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:23.190
So they stay with me,
and I hold their hand,

00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:24.510
and I seriously don't let go.

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:26.100
And the guys that you're gonna meet,

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:27.990
they'll confirm that
I'm obsessed with them.

00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:30.210
Like, I want them to stay with me forever.

00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:31.950
We do have a couple positions

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:35.610
that are always open for
individuals to come and try.

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:37.590
The interview process is the hardest part

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:39.420
of being an autistic adult.

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:41.130
You get nervous, freak out.

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:42.060
You don't know how to communicate.

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:43.140
You need a little bit more time.

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:46.050
So for me, I leave two spots
open for people to come in

00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:47.640
wherever they are on the spectrum.

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:49.800
And they can work on skills,

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:52.020
they can work on a job for the summer,

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:54.000
because they have a
dream job that they want,

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:56.010
like work on their resume.

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:57.300
I am like a one-stop shop.

00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:59.130
Like, just come to me.
We'll make it happen.

00:02:59.130 --> 00:02:59.980
- Yeah.
- But...

00:03:00.847 --> 00:03:02.280
- [Kelly] That's so
great. It's like a home.

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:04.630
(audience applauding)

00:03:04.630 --> 00:03:08.160
- Like, I realized that
more people with autism,

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:11.250
I wanted to help more people
and I wanted to employ them,

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:12.720
and I couldn't figure out how to do it.

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:13.980
And then I realized, a lot of people

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:17.580
that were messaging me were
artists and graphic designers.

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:19.950
And I said, "Well, I
have an art background.

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:22.110
I used to own an art
gallery. You can do this."

00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:25.980
And so I put out a recruitment

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:28.500
for autistic artists to
submit their artwork for me.

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:31.920
And it's all wearable art,
so you can go to our website.

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:34.110
You can see all the
autistic artists' work.

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:37.140
And I paid for all of their
designs, so they get paid,

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:39.930
and with every item
sold, they get royalties.

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:42.026
So I just printed more.
- That's incredible.

00:03:42.026 --> 00:03:43.284
(audience applauding)

00:03:43.284 --> 00:03:44.317
Like, that's important, right?

00:03:44.317 --> 00:03:45.803
The important part of
it is also getting paid.

00:03:45.803 --> 00:03:47.760
- Payment is, without a doubt,

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:49.200
the most important thing to me.

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:51.903
And the spectrum is large.

00:03:53.490 --> 00:03:54.870
When you meet someone with autism,

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:56.550
you've met just one person with autism.

00:03:56.550 --> 00:03:59.700
So there's all kinds of
people on the spectrum,

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:01.920
but some people need more support,

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:03.330
and maybe they don't quite get

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:05.790
that they just made 15 bucks that hour.

00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:07.980
But maybe when they go to the store later

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:09.960
and they buy something like...

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:12.450
Sorry, they realize that they earned that.

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:13.410
- Yeah.
- And it's like

00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:14.910
so important to me.
(audience applauding)

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:18.382
- It really is important to
feel that you have purpose.

00:04:18.382 --> 00:04:22.140
Like, that impact, you will see
that written on their faces.

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:25.470
- Just valued friendship.
Someone you can count on.

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:27.180
But the communication, like, sure,

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:29.580
maybe you're not doing
the regular communication,

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:31.830
but everyone knows that we
all have each other's back,

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:35.460
and the world can be kind
of scary and ours isn't.

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:37.428
- Yeah. I love that.

00:04:37.428 --> 00:04:39.316
(audience cheering)
The Awesome Company focuses

00:04:39.316 --> 00:04:42.090
on hiring autistic employees
to screen-print T-shirts

00:04:42.090 --> 00:04:43.860
and other items that are made to order.

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:45.990
We have two of those staff
members joining us now.

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:48.775
Everybody, give a big
hello to Brian and Matt.

00:04:48.775 --> 00:04:50.883
(audience cheering)
(audience applauding)

00:04:50.883 --> 00:04:53.940
So, hello, how are y'all
doing? Thank you for coming.

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:55.110
- Thank you for having us.

00:04:55.110 --> 00:04:56.200
- Yeah, thanks for having us.
- Absolutely.

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:57.993
- Yeah.
- I like your shirt.

00:04:58.860 --> 00:04:59.869
Are these shirts y'all made?

00:04:59.869 --> 00:05:01.680
- Oh yeah, oh yeah.
They're pretty awesome.

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:02.733
- They're pretty awesome (chuckles).

00:05:02.733 --> 00:05:04.440
(Matt laughs)
I know, I gotta give it...

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:05.490
So, Matt, I'm gonna start with you.

00:05:05.490 --> 00:05:09.420
Like, how did you come across
this? The Awesome Company.

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:11.160
- So I was bouncing around from jobs,

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:12.780
trying to find something that stuck,

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:16.980
but nothing was open and
understanding of autism.

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:19.290
And then one day, I heard Jacquie
here was recruiting people

00:05:19.290 --> 00:05:20.190
on the spectrum.

00:05:20.190 --> 00:05:22.740
And so I thought, "I'll apply,"

00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:26.100
went in and I've been there
since, for five years now.

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:26.933
- Oh my gosh.

00:05:26.933 --> 00:05:28.438
(audience cheering)
(audience applauding)

00:05:28.438 --> 00:05:31.350
That has to change your
life. It changed my life.

00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:32.430
First of all, you surround yourself

00:05:32.430 --> 00:05:33.630
with people you wanna
work with on a daily,

00:05:33.630 --> 00:05:35.460
and then also just having
a job that you enjoy

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:36.750
and you feel purpose.

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:39.090
That had to change your life,
right? What is it like now?

00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:42.720
- It feels amazing just
waking up, going in there,

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:45.660
knowing that I'm gonna see
the same people every day.

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:49.230
And that if I'm having a
problem or a great day,

00:05:49.230 --> 00:05:52.950
I can always talk to Jacquie
or Brian, or whoever's there.

00:05:52.950 --> 00:05:54.690
- Yeah.
- It just made me feel happy

00:05:54.690 --> 00:05:57.060
to know that I found a new home.

00:05:57.060 --> 00:05:57.942
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:05:57.942 --> 00:05:58.800
(audience applauding)

00:05:58.800 --> 00:05:59.873
- I love it.
(Jacquie laughs)

00:05:59.873 --> 00:06:01.860
Well, Brian, now I wanna talk to you.

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:04.200
So what's a typical day like for you?

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:06.510
- Well, there is no typical
day at The Awesome Company.

00:06:06.510 --> 00:06:10.110
One day I could be out
passing out marketing bags

00:06:10.110 --> 00:06:12.810
to business districts,

00:06:12.810 --> 00:06:17.160
another day I could be cutting
film to print on shirts,

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:20.220
another day I could be
writing thank you notes.

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:22.980
We write these handwritten
thank you notes.

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:23.903
- [Kelly] Oh, that's awesome.

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:28.680
- [Brian] Penmanship is key.
It really is a lost art.

00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:29.513
- [Kelly] I know. It is.

00:06:29.513 --> 00:06:31.380
- It really is.
- We do thank you cards here.

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:32.642
I do that for people here in-

00:06:32.642 --> 00:06:33.840
- We decided-
- That's awesome too.

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:34.673
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:06:34.673 --> 00:06:37.080
- We decided that we wanted
to connect with our community

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:39.420
in a world that you kind of
feel distant from people.

00:06:39.420 --> 00:06:42.240
So every single person
that buys a T-shirt from us

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:44.280
or a garment custom apparel,

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:46.837
they receive a handwritten
note from us saying,

00:06:46.837 --> 00:06:49.440
"Thank you so much for
letting us earn your business

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:51.300
and please tell your friends about us."

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:53.280
And it really is a great job,

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:55.470
but also, we're connecting
with the community

00:06:55.470 --> 00:06:57.450
and we're getting so much good love back,

00:06:57.450 --> 00:06:58.470
and it's just wonderful.

00:06:58.470 --> 00:07:01.350
So you have the best penmanship, I think.

00:07:01.350 --> 00:07:02.695
- Oh yeah, absolutely.

00:07:02.695 --> 00:07:04.389
(audience laughing)
Oh yeah.

00:07:04.389 --> 00:07:07.264
(audience applauding)

00:07:07.264 --> 00:07:08.880
- Well, Matt does some design work,

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:10.920
and he made a special piece
that he wanted to share.

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:13.079
So is this-
- Oh yes.

00:07:13.079 --> 00:07:14.610
We have a little surprise for you, so-

00:07:14.610 --> 00:07:15.570
- Oh, it's for me.

00:07:15.570 --> 00:07:16.920
- It's for you.

00:07:16.920 --> 00:07:18.060
- Okay.
- Right when we found out

00:07:18.060 --> 00:07:19.687
that we were gonna be here, I said,

00:07:19.687 --> 00:07:21.180
"Matt, you gotta make her a drawing."

00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:22.650
And we researched what you loved.

00:07:22.650 --> 00:07:24.540
So we realized you liked

00:07:24.540 --> 00:07:25.703
sunflowers.
- Cobalt blue.

00:07:25.703 --> 00:07:26.670
- Yes.
- Cobalt blue

00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:27.507
is her favorite.
- I love cobalt blue.

00:07:27.507 --> 00:07:29.040
- [Jacquie] And we heard that
you might be a good singer.

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:31.767
- You did research.
That's a specific color.

00:07:31.767 --> 00:07:34.410
- So, Matt made this for you.

00:07:34.410 --> 00:07:35.824
- Oh my gosh, I love it.
- Aw.

00:07:35.824 --> 00:07:37.190
- It's sunflower.
(audience applauding)

00:07:37.190 --> 00:07:38.023
- But...

00:07:38.023 --> 00:07:40.672
- With the music notes.

00:07:40.672 --> 00:07:42.680
Oh, more?
- And...

00:07:42.680 --> 00:07:47.010
- And to show what we do at
The Awesome Company is we put

00:07:47.010 --> 00:07:49.320
that artwork on some
clothes for you to wear.

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:51.051
- Oh my gosh, thank you.

00:07:51.051 --> 00:07:52.160
(audience applauding)

00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:53.194
I wanna see it.
- So now you have a little bit

00:07:53.194 --> 00:07:54.930
of The Awesome Company with you.

00:07:54.930 --> 00:07:57.570
- Oh my God, I'm gonna open
it now. I'm so excited.

00:07:57.570 --> 00:07:59.610
This is so awesome.

00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:01.173
Well, awesome. I didn't mean to say that.

00:08:01.173 --> 00:08:02.873
(Matt laughs)
- Yeah.

00:08:02.873 --> 00:08:05.307
- That is so nice.

00:08:05.307 --> 00:08:07.497
- You're welcome.
- Oh my gosh.

00:08:07.497 --> 00:08:10.200
Ah, well, I have something
for all of you as well.

00:08:10.200 --> 00:08:11.730
So our season-long partner, Pilot Pen,

00:08:11.730 --> 00:08:14.190
wants to celebrate people
creating fearlessly

00:08:14.190 --> 00:08:16.260
and awesomely like all of you.

00:08:16.260 --> 00:08:18.540
So they're hooking you
up with all sorts of pens

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:20.520
and supplies to help empower the artist

00:08:20.520 --> 00:08:23.250
within and behind The Awesome Company.

00:08:23.250 --> 00:08:25.620
And also, I just believe in this so much,

00:08:25.620 --> 00:08:27.600
and I just am so proud of everything

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:29.490
that is being accomplished
here, and all the purpose,

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:32.070
and the impact on the community
I can only imagine as well.

00:08:32.070 --> 00:08:33.998
So I'm gonna give $5,000 just to help out.

00:08:33.998 --> 00:08:35.332
(Jacquie screams)
(drum roll thumps)

00:08:35.332 --> 00:08:37.231
(cymbal clangs)
(audience cheering)

00:08:37.231 --> 00:08:39.567
I think it's so amazing.

00:08:39.567 --> 00:08:42.030
And I heard that you're
wanting to do a gallery, right?

00:08:42.030 --> 00:08:44.430
- So that's what I was
just gonna say is like-

00:08:44.430 --> 00:08:45.480
- I heard about the gallery

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:46.950
thing too.
- The universe is, like,

00:08:46.950 --> 00:08:50.430
exploding my soul open
right now because I thought,

00:08:50.430 --> 00:08:53.550
we have our autistic artists
and we have the wearable art,

00:08:53.550 --> 00:08:57.510
but I don't see any art
gallery that's dedicated

00:08:57.510 --> 00:08:58.800
to autistic artists only,

00:08:58.800 --> 00:08:59.790
so I'm opening one.

00:08:59.790 --> 00:09:02.100
- Oh my gosh, this is
incredible. I heard about that.

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:04.200
So I'm very excited about that.

00:09:04.200 --> 00:09:05.280
- So we are gonna use this.

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:07.380
We're gonna use the pens
and money to help with that.

00:09:07.380 --> 00:09:09.030
Thank you so much.
- Oh my God. Absolutely.

00:09:09.030 --> 00:09:09.900
Well, everybody, we need

00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:10.920
a short break.
- Hot eyeballs.

00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:11.910
- You're doing great.

00:09:11.910 --> 00:09:14.393
When you're crying, it
means it matters. It's okay.

